  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "gfjalZVUtuU4lytHkXMfgrLLI0k=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:07.631Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The tool field for Halcom names no partner platform at all \u2014 it records the firm's own payments and e-signature products. Its v2 verticals are banking and payments, so the products and the sectors line up: this is a company selling its own software into the market it knows.\n\nThat matters for how the priority-4 rank should be read. The rank came from a mechanical rescore of a provisional v2 tail row using size and slot occupancy, and where the platform slot is occupied by a firm's own IP, occupancy is not a temporary condition that a good pitch resolves. It is the business model. The flag consequently defers to an OSS-portfolio screen before anything is acted on.\n\nCommercially, a payments and e-signature vendor in banking is more plausibly a counterparty than a delivery channel: any component it adopted would have to earn a place on its own product roadmap. Confirming which of those two relationships is available is the point of running the screen first.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**4 \u00b7 needs OSS screen**\n- Halcom (SI)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> DERIVED, NOT SCORED ON v3 \u2014 v2 row rescored mechanically (size + slot-occupancy only). Run the OSS-portfolio screen before acting. v2 verticals: Banking, payments",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:27:07.631022Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:07.631027Z"
    }
  },
